## Overview

This week I focused mainly on Windows administration, system utilities, troubleshooting tools, operating system features, and the Windows command line.

The main goal was to understand what the tools actually do in a real IT environment instead of only memorizing their names for the exam.

---

## Windows Editions and Features

Reviewed the differences between Windows editions and when certain features are available.

### Key points

- Windows Home is aimed primarily at normal consumer use.
- Windows Pro includes business features such as:
  - Active Directory domain join
  - BitLocker
  - Group Policy
  - Remote Desktop hosting
- A Windows Home computer cannot simply be configured to join an Active Directory domain. It needs to be upgraded to an edition such as Windows Pro.

I also reinforced the difference between being an RDP client and an RDP host.

---

## Workgroups vs Domains

Reviewed how Windows systems can be organized in a network.

### Workgroup

- Peer-to-peer
- Each computer manages its own users and permissions
- No central authentication server
- Better suited to small/simple networks

### Domain

- Centralized administration
- Uses Active Directory
- Users, computers, permissions, and policies can be managed centrally
- Users can authenticate using domain accounts

This helped me understand why companies use domains instead of configuring every workstation individually.

---

## Active Directory and Group Policy

Reviewed the purpose of Active Directory Domain Services and Group Policy.

Active Directory provides a centralized database for:

- users
- computers
- groups
- authentication
- permissions

Group Policy allows administrators to centrally configure settings on users and computers.

Important commands:

- `gpupdate` / `gpupdate /force` → refresh Group Policy
- `gpresult /r` → show which policies are currently applied

Key distinction:

`gpupdate` = apply/update policy  
`gpresult` = inspect the result

